  [1mThe fork:[0m how the environment gets chosen at runtime. This is the decision the whole design hangs off, and it's not
  cheap to reverse — it determines whether the repo gains a build step, and what "switching environments" costs someone
  later.

  - [1mEdit one line in [38;5;153msettings.js[0m — cheapest, zero new tooling. Costs a manual edit (and a risk of committing the wrong
    one) every switch. Easy to upgrade later.
  - [1mHostname detection in [38;5;153msettings.js[0m — switches automatically; [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m → dev, everything else → prod. No build step,
    no manual edit. Costs a hardcoded hostname→URL map in the file, and it's awkward if two environments share a host.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m — the "proper" answer, but it means adding a bundler and a build step to a repo that currently
    has neither. Rules out just opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m in a browser.

  [1mMy recommendation: hostname detection.[0m It's the only one that actually delivers "easier to change environments"
  without tooling — the switch happens by itself — and it stays a single file you can rewrite in five minutes if you
  later add a build step.
